   X-Name: Timothy C. May

   Actually, the iterated prisoner's dilemma does *not* require the same exact
   players in each round. For example, the "Russians" and the "Americans" may
   play the game in various ways, even thought the individual players change
   frequently.

But in that case, the "players" are acting as agents for their
governments, as their behavior is (more or less) under control of the
governments in question.

   Other "public choice" games show IPD behavior--markets, for
   example--without the players be exactly the same people.

Actually, markets don't require IPD behavior, since each player is
getting maximum benefit out of each transaction individually.  That's
why markets work so wonderfully well -- any idiot can play them
optimally.

   I recall reading about how IPD experiments were done where the game was
   iterated as usual, but the players were rotated at each round (that is,
   everybody played the same number of rounds, but never against the same
   person more than once). The results were similar to the normal version.

I'd bet that ALWAYS DEFECT would win strongly, but that the players
were so socially conditioned that none of them realized the value of
playing it.  Has anyone replicated this on a computer tournament?

The real question to be answered in deciding whether something is an
IPD is whether "defectors" can be identified and interacted with
again.  It doesn't matter whether one *normally* identified players,
only whether identification can be made in order to punish defectors.
In the case of driving, in order to induce IPD behavior, we are
required to have license plates.

Dale

