Date: Thu, 1 Apr 93 19:46:02 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: TECH: Entropic costs of computation

I agree with Robin Hanson that interactive tutorials are hard to do in a
format like this...we need blackboards, we need homework reading (PLEASE,
everybody interested in thread should at least read Bennett and Landauer's
article "The Fundamental Limits of Computation" in the July 1985
"Scientific American," if not the other books some of us have cited), and
we need the capability of heading off wrong interpretations before they get
out of hand.

Having said this, and having already posted perhaps too many time today
(anything to keep me from working on the "Pagan Witch Trial" report!), let
me add my final words on this topic (final for now, at least):

Derek Zahn writes:

>Hal Finney writes, in a very lucid and useful response:
......
>> You don't _have_ to invoke entropy to calculate how much heat is
>> dissipated by a particular mechanism.  That can be done by plain old
>> dynamics.  Look at the friction, look at the materials, look at the
>> motions and forces, and calculate the heating.  This will always give
>> you a correct answer.
>
>< THIS IS APPARENTLY THE IMPORTANT POINT >
>OK, but then WHY would "reversible" logic help?  In our rod logic
>computer, the heat dissipation is caused by the little rods smashing
>into each other, right?  The way to minimize this is to minimize the
>bashing, right?  How can a more complex design help?

Hal is certainly right that macroscopic scale machines--wristwatches,
motors, microprocessors, etc.--have heat dissipations easily calculated by
calculating a bunch of things about parts rubbing together, banging each
other, heating wires, etc. This is routine stuff.

The question Landauer, Bennett, Fredkin, Keyes, Toffoli, and others have
addressed is this:

* What is the *minimum* energy dissipation associated with information
processing?

Until around 1961, when Landauer wrote ""Irreversibility and Heat
Generation in the Computation Process," IBM J. Res. Dev., 5, just about
everybody *knew* that the minimum energy dissipation for a logic operation
was near kT--various "Maxwell's demon" papers by Brillouin, Szilard, and
others had "proved" this. 

This puts us in the domain of fractions of an eV in energy, which is around
kT. Today's smallest commercial electronic devices are switching
("wasting") many thousands of times this energy per logic operation.

The consensus these days by Landauer, Bennett, and others is that there is
no intrinsic limit on energy dissipation...that it depends on whether the
computation is reversible or irreversible. Reversible computations may in
principle dissipate much less than kT. The key to reversibility lies in
storing enough information that the computation can be run backwards.

(It is not clear to me, nor do others seem to have achieved final
conclusions, if any large computations that reach "new"
conclusions--presumably the reason to do computations in the first
place--can be done reversibly on a global scale. However, it may certainly
be the case that parts of a computation are reversible and parts are
not...some have speculated that the brain achieves a high computational
throughput on a "better than kT" basis, perhaps by using locally reversible
computations.)

The significance for this list is that nanotechnology may very well both
*need* such computational approaches and also make them *possible*. The
need comes from the incredible number of nanotech logic gates per unit
volume in some proposed systems and the high heat generation implied by
"normal" computational approaches.

That nanotech may make such approaches possible, or practical, lies in the
rod-logic computers proposed by Drexler. Like Fredkin's billiard ball logic
gate, these may be reversible. Conventional circuits may be designable
using reversible logic--Ralph Merkle and others claim to have some designs.
(There has been little interest in this for the very practical reason that
energy dissipation of cuurent designs is already orders of magnitude
greater, as described above.)

>I do understand, by the way, that these are only "theoretical" minimums
>requiring actual designs to reach them -- but now it seems that the
>minimums are MUCH less than I'd thought.

Yes, by orders of magnitude.

>> But you can't necessarily restart the calculation unless you
>> remembered what all the inputs were.  A machine which transforms
>> inputs into outputs will not necessarily record that information.

Most computational models do not save intermediate results, making reversal
impossible.

>>From all this, it appears that the answer to the puzzle is simply
>that we're going to have friction in our nanocomputer, and we should
>try to minimize it.  This is hardly a profound or deep thought!
>It's exactly what I'd expect!  Talk of limits due to information
>theory are sort of red herrings, aren't they?

No, for the reasons described above. Gross issues like friction dissipation
in a rod-logic computer can be made arbitrarily small by running the
computer very slowly...fundamental dissipation cannot be gotten rid of.

It seems clear that some computations are reversible and can be made to
dissipate arbitrarily small amounts of energy. Some logical operations,
AND, OR, etc., are not reversible, ipso facto. Others, like NOT, are. The
trick is to design algorithms using logically reversible operations.

Whether large-scale computations, such as might be found in one of
Drexler's general assembler "breadboxes," can be made to use mostly
reversible logic is an open question.

-Tim May

