Subject: Traffic guide and game theory

Nickpicking mode ON!

>   From: tribble@memex.com (E. Dean Tribble)
>
>   This is much more analogous to the iterated prisoner's dilemna rather
>   than just the prisoner's dilemna. . . . The
>   incentives change dramatically in the iterated case.
>
>True, but that's only if the same players iterate.  Or rather, if one
>has a change to build a reputation.
>
>Dale

I think it is most accurate to say that the incentives change dramatically
only if the prisoner's dilemma (PD) is *indefinitely* iterated.  See
Axelrod, THE EVOLUTION OF COOPERATION, for an explanation of how PDs that
players know will terminate in a set number of iterated rounds present the
same incentives as a PD that runs for only one round.

Focusing on reputation gets closer to the important factors and puts things
in user-friendly terminology.  But let's not forget that reputations won't
affect behavior in a PD unless the players plan to run into their
counterparts some indefinite number of times in the future.  (Outside the
formal constraints of a PD reputations play other important roles,
however.)

