Subject: TECH: Entropic costs of computation

I agree 90% with what Tim posted, but there is one area I'd like to
clarify.  I am no expert on this although I've read several papers on
it.  But that was over a year ago and I won't have access to my records
until Sunday.

From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)

> Hal is certainly right that macroscopic scale machines--wristwatches,
> motors, microprocessors, etc.--have heat dissipations easily calculated by
> calculating a bunch of things about parts rubbing together, banging each
> other, heating wires, etc. This is routine stuff.
> [...]
> Gross issues like friction dissipation
> in a rod-logic computer can be made arbitrarily small by running the
> computer very slowly...fundamental dissipation cannot be gotten rid of.

Well, I elided so much material that I'm not sure of Tim's position,
but I want to make it clear that Newtonian physics still holds at the
level of molecular motion (neglecting QM, which you can usually do at
this scale).  Molecules interact and bounce off each other, thereby
transmitting heat.  They are influenced by various potential fields which
may restrict their motion.  But the patterns of their motions can be
analyzed in Newtonian terms successfully.

What this implies is that you _can_ calculate how much heat will be given
off by a particular mechanical mechanism moving in a particular way,
just by looking at local inter-atomic forces, without regard to any
considerations of information theory.  No molecules are going to start
moving non-Newtonianly just because some global constraints imply that
a certain amount of heat will be dissipated.  The brute-force, local,
molecule-by-molecule calculations will always be correct.

The role of the information-theory considerations is to guarantee that
when you analyze energy dissipation using such a brute-force
calculation, you are going to get an answer at least as large as the
information-theoretic minimum.  In many cases the detailed calculation
is too hard to do.  You can still look at the information and entropy
analysis and get a lower bound which can be useful (and which is
amazingly high for some of these nanomachines).

Information-theory and mechanical analyses are complementary.  They will
never contradict each other.  The information-theory dissipation does not
have to be "added in" after we've calculated all the "conventional" mechanical
forms of dissipation.  Rather, the inforomation-theory calculation tells us
that when we do the mechanical calculation we will end up with a value greater
than some bound.

It's like the use of potential energy in simple dynamics calculations
in high school physics.  You can do a detailed analysis using forces,
accelerations, and velocities to get an answer.  Or you can often get a
"big picture" result just by looking at energies which will show
whether a certain configuration can occur or not.  But these analyses
will never contradict each other.  You never have to calculate the
detailed dynamics in terms of forces and such and then "add in" a
potential energy correction.  Rather, you can be confident that nothing
you calculate with your force analysis will contradict the limits you
can (often more easily) establish using energy arguments.

Well, like I said, I'm not sure Tim or anyone else would disagree with this,
but this is how I see the information-theory arguments fitting in.

Hal

