I agree with Hal's comments...I won't "quote and comment" on his post, to
save the sanity of readers and writer alike.

My point about the gross effects of gears hitting gears, parts rubbing,
wires heating up, etc.,  was only that these effects--whether macroscopic
or microscopic--are much larger for the machines around us than the effects
Bennett, Landauer, et. al. are talking about.

I felt the discussion had drifted into a discussion of total, practical
energy dissipation of real world machines and was not addressing the real
issue of "fundamental limits" (which I certainly believed was implicit in
Derek's choice of the word "entropic").

Final comments, mostly directed to Ray:

I'm sorry to hear your region of the world is so impoverished. But there
must be other universities nearby whose libraries you can simple walk
into...I do this all the time (mostly at Stanford). I'm fortunate to have a
library card at UCSC, here in Santa Cruz, which has a huge new
glass-and-redwood Science Library with every journal imaginable (well, I
can imagine a lot, as Han Solo used to say). And over the mountain, in
Silicon Valley, are a dozen sources of technical books, including the huge
"Computer Literacy" chain, Stacey's, and the most incredible bookstore I
have ever seen, the cavernous Stanford Bookstore--widely recognized, even
by the "Wall Street Journal," as the largest seller of academic and
scientific books in the U.S. and perhaps in the world.

Unfortunately, such bookstores are not kind on my wallet.

Other points:

* the Cover book, while wonderful, says almost nothing about these issues.
It is not the place to look for stuff on this.

* "Nanosystems" is an OK book, and is certainly better than the
techno-fluff about nanotech in "Unbounding the Future," but it is meant for
someone with a heavy chemistry background. I bought it, of course, but
don't have the time or interest (I have no professional interest, that is,
in spending the necessary hundreds of hours working my way through
it...what will readers do if and when they finish it...apply for jobs as
nanotechnology machine designers?).

* the 1985 Sci Am paper may not have a lot of equations in it, as you
noted, but it has *good explanations*, which is much more important.
Equations are not even really needed for what's involved here. I urge you
to reread it.

* Bennett also wrote a 1987 paper, "Demons, Engines, and the Second Law,"
Sci Am, Nov. '87.

* Every university library *must* have back issues of "Nature" (if yours
doesn't, transfer immediately). You can read about algorithmic information
theory and logical depth in Zurek, "Thermodynamic Cost of Computation,
Algorithmic Complexity, and the Information Metric," Nature, 341
(1989);119-124.

Positively my last comment on this thread!

